# Ooda CLI — Editor Setup

File: cli/docs/CLI_SETUP.oot

The `ooda` binary is the unified meta-router for the openOODA tactical toolchain. It has 4 in-process command groups and 3 external package spawns.

## In-process subcommands

| Subcommand | File | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `ooda build` | `cli/build/host_build.oo::run_oodac_build` | Compile a .oo file with oodac |
| `ooda run`   | `cli/build/host_build.oo::run_oodac_run`   | Compile and run a .oo file |
| `ooda gen`   | `cli/build/gen.oo::run_ooda_gen`           | Scaffold new .oo files |
| `ooda test`  | `cli/build/test.oo::run_ooda_test`         | Compile and run a single .oo test |
| `ooda install` | `cli/build/install.oo::run_ooda_install_cmd` | Install a pre-built pack |
| `ooda bench` | `cli/build/bench.oo::run_ooda_bench`       | Time oodac check/run |
| `ooda init`  | `cli/build/init.oo::cli_init_project`      | Bootstrap a project layout |
| `ooda context` | `cli/build/context.oo::run_ooda_context`  | List .oo files + emit AI codex |
| `ooda health`  | `cli/build/health.oo::run_ooda_health`    | Print ooda health: ok |
| `ooda fix`   | `cli/fix/fix.oo::run_ooda_fix`             | Check, ask, apply E_CAP/E_TC/E_PARSE/E_IMP/E_MUST edits |
| `ooda digest` | `cli/fix/digest.oo::run_ooda_digest`      | SHA-256 of a file |
| `ooda fmt`   | `cli/fmt/fmt.oo::run_ooda_fmt`             | Format .oo files in place |
| `ooda token` | `cli/token/cmd_token.oo::run_ooda_token`   | 12 token sub-subcommands (status, report, audit, query, ask, schema, diff, tail, fix, snapshot, enforce, extra) |

## External packages (spawned)

| Subcommand | Binary | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `ooda opm <sub>` | `opm` (from /opm/ repo) | Package manager (add, audit, ingest, launch, etc.) |
| `ooda lsp`        | `lsp` (from /lsp/ repo) | LSP server over stdio JSON-RPC |
| `ooda mcp`        | `mcp` (from /mcp/ repo) | MCP server over stdio JSON-RPC |

The `ooda` binary does NOT link these packages. It spawns them as subprocesses. The spawn lookup order is:
1. `$OODACODEX/bin/<name>` (the opm-managed install dir)
2. `$PATH` (system install)

Stdio is inherited. Editor stdio flows through to `lsp` and `mcp` transparently.
